<pending_commit branch="master" change_id="I26f26b9643bfc0f6c40a0ae332ea975c582bfe8f" commit="bbae115d303946d63dafde7f2a21b78d0cda78ad" commit_message="Split logic to determine required_erase_size into its own function  This just splits code out of handle_partial_read() to make the caller (handle_partial_read) a bit cleaner and allow us to use the same logic in an upcoming patch.  BUG=none BRANCH=none TEST=tested w/ follow-up patch  Signed-off-by: David Hendricks &lt;dhendrix@chromium.org&gt; Change-Id: I26f26b9643bfc0f6c40a0ae332ea975c582bfe8f Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/320205 Reviewed-by: Shawn N &lt;shawnn@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="1" gerrit_number="320205" owner_email="dhendrix@chromium.org" pass_count="0" patch_number="1" project="chromiumos/third_party/flashrom" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/third_party/flashrom" ref="refs/changes/05/320205/1" remote="cros" total_fail_count="1"/><pending_commit branch="master" change_id="I9c38ef61d7ced192c0303422756741ab37b4f5de" commit="a75e567c90715a712b34f9a26c849a9e85dff59b" commit_message="Fix math bug in handle_partial_verify()  If required_erase_size does not get set (remains 0), the modulo operation blows up (floating point exception).  The bug is tickled when both --diff and --fast-verify are used since we didn't set required_erase_size in that particular case. --diff makes flashrom read from a file (which doesn't touch the chip and thus skips alignment logic) and then &quot;--fast-verify&quot; causes handle_partial_verify() to be called.  To avoid this problem, this patch makes it so that we set required_erase_size whenever a partial read/verify takes place.  BUG=none BRANCH=none TEST=used servo to write content to a region while employing &quot;--diff&quot; and &quot;--fast-verify&quot;  Change-Id: I9c38ef61d7ced192c0303422756741ab37b4f5de Signed-off-by: David Hendricks &lt;dhendrix@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/320206 Reviewed-by: Shawn N &lt;shawnn@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="1" gerrit_number="320206" owner_email="dhendrix@chromium.org" pass_count="0" patch_number="2" project="chromiumos/third_party/flashrom" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/third_party/flashrom" ref="refs/changes/06/320206/2" remote="cros" total_fail_count="1"/><pending_commit branch="chromeos-3.14" change_id="I8f5f77550953c22dbe5473cc30e97d2d6595ca35" commit="06f04756410645ff2ef1be88268a67c6e1fc2f74" commit_message="BACKPORT: HID: fix out of bound access in extract() and implement()  extract() and implement() access buffer containing reports in 64-bit chunks, but there is no guarantee that buffers are padded to 64 bit boundary. In fact, KASAN has caught such OOB access with i2c-hid and Synaptics touch controller.  Instead of trying to hunt all parties that allocate buffers and make sure they are padded, let's switch extract() and implement() to byte access. It is a bit slower, bit we are not dealing with super fast devices here.  Also let's fix link to the HID spec while we are at it.  Signed-off-by: Dmitry Torokhov &lt;dtor@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-by: Benjamin Tissoires &lt;benjamin.tissoires@redhat.com&gt; Signed-off-by: Jiri Kosina &lt;jkosina@suse.cz&gt;  BUG=chromium:569967 TEST=Boot ryu, verify keyboard and touchscreen work; use KASAN.  Conflicts:  drivers/hid/hid-core.c due to extract() being called hid_extract_field() and being exported upstream.  (cherry-picked from 5137b354bc8a5c04edb10d83f8bdb0bf8896fd68) Change-Id: I8f5f77550953c22dbe5473cc30e97d2d6595ca35 Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/322222 Commit-Ready: Dmitry Torokhov &lt;dtor@chromium.org&gt; Tested-by: Dmitry Torokhov &lt;dtor@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-by: Douglas Anderson &lt;dianders@chromium.org&gt; (cherry picked from commit a4729ee8d2d242a7f8eb230a21f7b4f8d5bf6bb2) Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/322760 " fail_count="0" gerrit_number="322760" owner_email="dtor@chromium.org" pass_count="0" patch_number="1" project="chromiumos/third_party/kernel" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/third_party/kernel" ref="refs/changes/60/322760/1" remote="cros" total_fail_count="0"/><pending_commit branch="chromeos-3.18" change_id="I21d148067c0417eb6442454928c64ac28735eb7b" commit="feca9ac816b782099465f53e909c56fc73480d5c" commit_message="CHROMIUM: drm/nouveau/gr/gf100: handle compute sw method properly  We can observe the illegal mthd error when running some benchmarks:  nouveau E[  PGRAPH][57000000.gpu] ILLEGAL_MTHD ch 15 [0x04020be000 RenderThread[2208]] subc 1 class 0xb1c0 mthd 0x1528 data 0x00000000  Although we do support the sw method for compute class, we still hit the problem. That's because the gr intr handler couldn't get the correct handle from the namedb which has only 3d class. This patch adds an additional path for the compute class to handle the set shader exception method.  Change-Id: I21d148067c0417eb6442454928c64ac28735eb7b Signed-off-by: Vince Hsu &lt;vince.h@nvidia.com&gt; Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/315371 Tested-by: David Ung &lt;davidu@nvidia.com&gt; Reviewed-by: David Ung &lt;davidu@nvidia.com&gt; Reviewed-by: Stéphane Marchesin &lt;marcheu@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="0" gerrit_number="315371" owner_email="vince.h@nvidia.com" pass_count="0" patch_number="3" project="chromiumos/third_party/kernel" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/third_party/kernel" ref="refs/changes/71/315371/3" remote="cros" total_fail_count="0"/><pending_commit branch="master" change_id="I7b74df102ba060a7bc35306192be41c5c59c9825" commit="52b1b07c125a27a341b0651ef0335c776702265d" commit_message="Implement policy_CookiesBlockedForUrls AutoTest  This test verifies the behavior of Chrome OS for a range of valid values of the CookiesBlockedForUrls user policy, with the DefaultCookiesSetting = 1 (i.e., allow cookies for all pages except for those with the URLs listed in the CookiesBlockedForUrls policy value).  BUG=chromium:573097 TEST=Run test_that &lt;IP&gt; policy_CookiesBlockedForUrls  Change-Id: I7b74df102ba060a7bc35306192be41c5c59c9825 Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/321860 Commit-Ready: Scott Cunningham &lt;scunningham@chromium.org&gt; Tested-by: Scott Cunningham &lt;scunningham@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-by: Scott Cunningham &lt;scunningham@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="1" gerrit_number="321860" owner_email="krishnargv@chromium.org" pass_count="0" patch_number="2" project="chromiumos/third_party/autotest" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/third_party/autotest" ref="refs/changes/60/321860/2" remote="cros" total_fail_count="1"/><pending_commit branch="master" change_id="Id8d5c64d9f27d1aa50f559b9bc1ac19c3b8ef849" commit="b2a55014e5fcd25144491c7e33b3a48c31c23f7c" commit_message="smbios: Expose info-getting SMBIOS functions and use them  This patch does a couple things: - Removes const-ness from the return type of sys_cb members dealing   with system info retrieval. - Exposes generic SMBIOS info getter functions. - Removes a lot of trivial wrappers used in board code to call SMBIOS   info getter functions.  BUG=none BRANCH=none TEST=compiled for x86 and ARM, &quot;mosys platform&quot; commands still work on cyan and veyron_danger  Change-Id: Id8d5c64d9f27d1aa50f559b9bc1ac19c3b8ef849 Signed-off-by: David Hendricks &lt;dhendrix@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/320778 Reviewed-by: Shawn N &lt;shawnn@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="0" gerrit_number="320778" owner_email="dhendrix@chromium.org" pass_count="0" patch_number="1" project="chromiumos/platform/mosys" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/platform/mosys" ref="refs/changes/78/320778/1" remote="cros" total_fail_count="0"/><pending_commit branch="master" change_id="I4d895c0bfb2710e9ec4ee020f6655bc993bbc5ce" commit="6e07ba3da24df9e293c07610df